<p>Slovenia&#x27;s parliamentary election ended in a near tie, with Prime Minister Robert Golob&#x27;s liberal Freedom Movement and Janez Jansa&#x27;s opposition Slovenian Democratic Party running almost level. That left the 90-seat National Assembly without a clear majority and made coalition-building the decisive next step. Exit polls had pointed to a narrow lead for Freedom Movement, but near-complete preliminary counts showed the race tightening into an almost perfect deadlock. The result opens a new round of political bargaining in the European Union member state, with smaller parties likely to determine who forms the next government. The campaign itself was highly polarized, and several outlets highlighted disputes over alleged corruption material, a report about contacts with Israeli private intelligence firm Black Cube, and warnings from campaigners about anti-Romany rhetoric. Even with the final coalition still unclear, the close contest also points to strong democratic engagement, with Slovenian voters clearly invested in choosing the country&#x27;s direction.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/JAsQlyYsjzswQqkh2CjXnCSmu4xxFjnlOeyssMWSKfyJUbH-cxkH12uhNRcAf9S4epdM0hdEWResVxgMLuFkIGQoph5fZmvJEKwkyTPUKkodfREgZDTCyYsNQnz95SU_W15GQVKH0yMySJ272JaI6Gh-DQ_ldGTeG0ItVsLnm6UZi9d0XBdCfoh5M0Gla3Zw_ah2gEj472FitGecOFXIrA' alt='Voters casting their ballots in Radomlje, Slovenia, on Sunday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Seat math: An exit poll cited by TV Slovenia and Pop TV put Freedom Movement at 29.9% and about 30 seats, down from 41 previously, while Jansa&#x27;s SDS was projected to win 27 seats.</li><li>No majority: Neither main party appeared close to an outright majority in the 90-member chamber, leaving smaller parties in a potential kingmaker role during coalition talks.</li><li>Campaign tone: Observers described the campaign as dirty and increasingly heated after covert videos purporting to show government corruption surfaced on an anonymous website.</li><li>Minority rights: Campaigners told The Guardian the election had become, in part, a referendum on how Slovenia treats Roma communities amid a rise in anti-Romany rhetoric.</li></ul><blockquote>If someone wants a government like the one we’ve had so far, then they are probably satisfied with what these parallel results indicate. - Janez Jansa</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Janez Jansa: After exit polls were released, Jansa said voters who wanted change would need to wait for the final results before judging the outcome, and that his camp had done everything in its power. (<a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/slovenias-freedom-movement-set-to-win-vote-exit-poll/'>Michael West</a>)</li><li>Campaigners on Roma issues: Campaigners warned that anti-Romany rhetoric had surged during the race and said the vote had become a test of how Slovenia treats one of its most marginalized communities. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/22/slovenia-election-voting-campaign-claims-anti-romany-rhetoric'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>International media analysts: Coverage across outlets emphasized that coalition-building, not just the raw vote winner, will decide who governs because the assembly is fragmented. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/22/world/europe/election-in-slovenia-will-test-europes-political-mood.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/slovenia-election-sees-ruling-party-tied-with-opposition/a-76473130'>Slovenia election sees ruling party tied with opposition</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://michaelwest.com.au/slovenias-freedom-movement-set-to-win-vote-exit-poll/'>Slovenia’s Freedom Movement set to win vote: exit poll</a> - michaelwest.com.au</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/22/slovenias-freedom-movement-party-takes-narrow-election-lead-exit-poll'>Slovenia’s liberals and conservatives neck and neck in parliamentary vote</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.pbs.org/newshour/world/slovenias-governing-liberals-and-right-wing-populists-neck-and-neck-preliminary-results-show'>Slovenia&#x27;s governing liberals and right-wing populists neck and neck, preliminary results show</a> - pbs.org</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/22/world/europe/election-in-slovenia-will-test-europes-political-mood.html'>Election in Slovenia Is a Near Tie</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/slovenias-governing-liberals-face-wing-populists-tight-parliamentary-131297814'>Slovenia&#x27;s liberals and populists neck and neck, preliminary results show</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/22/slovenia-election-voting-campaign-claims-anti-romany-rhetoric'>Slovenia goes to polls in election marked by claims of anti-Romany rhetoric</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/slovenians-vote-tight-race-between-liberals-populist-right-2026-03-22/'>Slovenians vote for 90 seats in the National Assembly. A tight race is forecasted between the incumbent prime minister Robert Golob and his predecessor Janez Janša.</a> - reuters.com</li></ul>